ent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y,\\,-x,\\,-1⟩=⟨8,2\\pi ,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2c\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-cy &=8 \\\\ -xc &=2\\pi \\\\ -c &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-x(-2)=2\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=\\pi .\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $x=\\pi $ into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(-2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -(-2)y=8\\,⟹\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\pi $ and $y=2$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(\\pi )(2) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(\\pi ,2,-2\\pi ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186323964 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-xy) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-y \\\\ \\fra