 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186326798 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186326799 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $$\n\\displaystyle x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186326799 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186326803 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{x}\n$$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186326803 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}-x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{x},\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{1}{x}$ and $\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0+0}{1-0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{1} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186326807 1071 "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=17,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186326807 1071 "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=17,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186326816 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y+z}2xyz\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186326816 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{z}{\\int }_{0}^{y+z}2xyz\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186326825 1074 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $1+5+25+125+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{7}+\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{56}{9}+\\frac{784}{27}+⋯\n$$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\\end{gathered}\n$$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.01}{0.1} \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}0.1+0.01+0.001+0.000\\,1+⋯\