 inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186335878 1071 "What is the domain of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?\n$$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186335878 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186335878 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $$\n\\displaystyle -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\f